--- body ---
## Context

Merged 2026-09-03 without waiting on these: none of them affect any current
verdict or committed artifact, and each requires a condition this codebase
doesn't currently produce. Bundled here rather than filed as five tickets,
per the severity floor — none alone clears the bar for a dedicated ticket.

## Findings

1. **0620 (`tests/test_index_schema_fixtures.py`)** — the module-level
   `pytestmark` applies `skipif(node missing)` to every test in the file,
   including the new leg-3 tests, which only need `git`. On a node-less
   machine holding a valid `upstream.git/` mirror, the new leg silently
   SKIPs instead of reporting NOT-RUN — the exact "skip and pass are the
   same colour" defect this ticket exists to close, one layer up. `make
   schema-gate` (added by the same PR) sidesteps this for its own strict
   run, but the plain suite doesn't. Fix: exempt the leg-3 tests from the
   node skipif, or split them into their own module.

2. **0620 (same file)** — `check_declaration_against_upstream`'s regex
   takes the *first* `^const SCHEMA_VERSION` match in the upstream file.
   An unprefixed historical value inside a `/* */` block comment (sitting
   at column 0) ahead of the live declaration would be read as the answer.
   Not live today (today's upstream file has no such comment), cheap fix:
   assert exactly one match, or take the last one.

3. **0621 (`bench/smoke_upstream.py`)** — `_rrf_rank_of` has no upper bound
   on the recovered rank. Below roughly score < 0.0164, 6-decimal rounding
   becomes coarser than the spacing between consecutive RRF values, so an
   increasing fraction of ordinary weak similarity scores get accepted as
   "proven RRF rank," defeating the diagnostic's purpose for that range.
   Informational field only — doesn't move any current pass/fail, and no
   committed artifact has a score in that range. Fix: bound `rank` (e.g.
   `rank <= limit * K` for a generous K).

4. **0621 (same file)** — a NaN score crashes the whole script (uncaught
   `ValueError` in `round(1/float(score))`), losing the JSON artifact for
   every check, not just this one. Mechanism confirmed; whether a real
   server can put a literal NaN on the wire is not established. Cheap fix:
   `math.isnan`/`math.isinf` guard before the round().

5. **0624 (`bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py`)** — the README row and
   two code comments claim the work-counter derivation covers "any build
   that starts reporting counters... with no edit to the adapter." The
   actual mechanism only recognizes counters under the literal top-level
   keys `work`/`counters` in two specific RPC replies (`WORK_OBJECT_KEYS`).
   A build that groups counters under a third key name, or nests one level
   deeper, is silently read as `None` — the same overclaim shape this
   ticket was filed to close, relocated into the fix's own wording. Fix:
   narrow the three claims to name the actual key set and replies, or
   widen `_work_counters` to match what's claimed.

6. **0626 (`tests/test_acceptance_adapter_{zotseek,zotero_core_6012,zoteus}.py`)**
   — none of the three new posture-refusal tests check that `wrap()`'s
   *return value* is actually used. If a future edit dropped `argv =
   self._posture.wrap(...)` to a bare `self._posture.wrap(...)`, all three
   refusal tests would stay green (refusal raises unconditionally before
   returning anything), but a real *accepted* posture would silently spawn
   the target unwrapped — the identity-boundary breach tickets 0625/0626
   exist to prevent. Confirmed empirically. Pre-existing gap across all
   five adapters (including beaver/zotero_mcp), not introduced by 0626;
   out of that ticket's stated scope (mirrors an existing refusal-only
   pattern) but worth closing once, for all five adapters together.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Each finding above is fixed, or explicitly deferred again with a
      reason, one at a time or in whatever grouping makes sense
